WEBVTT

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:07.260
This is what we build on Microsoft
Azure for Dental Data.

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:13.680
One rule guides the design: Microsoft inside the
node, open standards at the border.

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:16.880
In the practice, the X-ray leaves
the unit through an open

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:21.640
source DICOM gateway, Orthanc, and is
pseudonymised at source.

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:24.160
In the data node, Azure
Health Data Services

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:28.440
stores the record in FHIR and
the image in DICOM.

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:32.400
Microsoft Fabric and OneLake prepare
the data for analysis.

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:36.680
Security comes from Microsoft Entra
ID, Azure Key Vault,

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:40.100
Microsoft Defender for Cloud and
Microsoft Sentinel.

00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:43.560
At the border, the Eclipse EDC
connector works,

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:45.620
deployed on Azure Kubernetes Service.

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:49.360
It publishes the catalogue in
HealthDCAT-AP,

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:54.900
negotiates ODRL contracts and identifies
itself with Gaia-X credentials.

00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:57.120
The artificial intelligence
is trained with

00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:00.180
Azure Machine Learning and
federated learning:

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:04.580
the model travels to each practice and
the data stay where they are.

00:01:04.630 --> 00:01:08.360
Confidential computing protects the
data even during processing,

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:13.080
and Microsoft Foundry writes draft
reports that the dentist reviews.

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:16.920
So every X-ray follows a safe
and controlled path,

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:22.530
from the practice to the model, without any
identifiable data leaving the node.
